        content: `# The Thin Line Between Assistance and Plagiarism

The use of Artificial Intelligence in academia is the debate of the century. While some universities ban it completely, others are starting to integrate it.

If you are looking for an "academic paper writer" who does everything for you, **this article is not for you**. But if you want to use technology to enhance your learning without compromising ethics, read on.

---

## 🚫 What you must NEVER do (Red Zone)

1.  **Copy and paste entire texts:** This is plagiarism, even if generated by a machine. Detection tools (like Turnitin) are advancing fast.
2.  **Invent bibliography:** AI hallucinations are common. ChatGPT can invent papers that do not exist. If you cite them, you will be caught.
3.  **Not declaring its use:** Transparency is key. If you used AI, say so.

## ✅ Ethical and Recommended Uses (Green Zone)

### 1. As a Socratic Tutor
Instead of asking "write an essay on Kant", ask: *"Explain Kant's categorical imperative with modern examples to see if I understood it correctly"*.

### 2. To overcome "Blank Page Syndrome"
Ask for an outline or structure. *"I'm going to write about climate change. What 5 key points should I not forget?"*.

### 3. Bibliography Review (with specialized tools)
Do not use ChatGPT to search for papers. Use:
*   **Consensus:** Searches a database of 200M papers.
*   **Elicit:** Analyzes papers and extracts conclusions.
*   **Scite.ai:** Verifies if citations are real and if the paper supports or refutes the claim.

### 4. Style and grammar correction
It is totally legitimate to use AI to clean up your writing, just as you use a spell checker.

---

## 🛠️ Tools for the Ethical Researcher

| Tool | Main Function | Is it safe? |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **Perplexity** | Search with real citations | ✅ Yes (verify sources) |
| **Grammarly** | Grammar correction | ✅ Yes |
| **Paperpal** | Specific editor for academic writing | ✅ Yes |
| **Hugging Face (Detectors)** | To check your own text | ⚠️ Use only as reference |

---

## 📝 How to cite AI (APA 7 Standards)

If you used AI to generate text, you must cite it.

**Format:**
> OpenAI. (2026). *ChatGPT* (Version GPT-4) [Large language model]. https://chat.openai.com

**In text:**
> "As the AI model ChatGPT suggests (OpenAI, 2026)..."

### Conclusion

AI is a calculator for letters. Use it to process information, not to avoid thinking. Your university degree certifies that YOU have the knowledge, not that you know how to use a prompt.`
